gbw -- compute a Gröbner basis with respect to a weight vector
**************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        gbI = gbw(I, w)
        gbM = gbw(M, w)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, in the Weyl algebra
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, with entries in the Weyl
        algebra
      * w, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of weights
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note thing: (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, default value null, if
        set to homogenize the input is homogenized in the homogeneous Weyl
        algebra
  * Outputs:
      * gbI, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, with the generators forming
        a Gröbner basis of the ideal with respect to the weight vector
      * gbM, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, with the columns forming a
        Gröbner basis of the submodule generated by the columns of the matrix
        with respect to the weight vector

Description
===========

This routine computes a Gröbner basis of a left ideal I of the Weyl algebra
with respect to a weight vector w = (u,v) where  u+v >= 0. In the case where
u_i+v_i > 0 for all i, the ordinary Buchberger algorithm works for any term
order refining the weight order. If there exists i so that u_i+v_i = 0 the
Buchberger algorithm needs to be adapted to  guarantee termination. There are
two strategies for doing this. One is to homogenize to an ideal of the
homogeneous Weyl algebra.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : makeWA(QQ[x,y])                           |
|                                               |
|o1 = QQ[x..y, dx, dy]                          |
|                                               |
|o1 : PolynomialRing, 2 differential variable(s)|
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal (x*dx+2*y*dy-3, dx^2-dy)        |
|                                               |
|                                2              |
|o2 = ideal (x*dx + 2y*dy - 3, dx  - dy)        |
|                                               |
|o2 : Ideal of QQ[x..y, dx, dy]                 |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : gbw(I, {-1,-3,1,3})                       |
|                                               |
|                                  2            |
|o3 = ideal (x*dx + 2y*dy - 3, - dx  + dy)      |
|                                               |
|o3 : Ideal of QQ[x..y, dx, dy]                 |
+-----------------------------------------------+

The other is to homogenize with respect to the weight vector w. More details
can be found in [SST (see https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/pdf/1734566.pdf
), Sections 1.1 and 1.2].

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : gbw(I, {1,3,3,-1})                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|                                2                                2  2    2   |
|o4 = ideal (x*dx + 2y*dy - 3, dx  - dy, 2y*dx*dy + x*dy - 2dx, 4y dy  - x dy |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     + 2x*dx - 2y*dy)                                                        |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Ideal of QQ[x..y, dx, dy]                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The weight vector w = (u,v) must have u+v>=0.

See also
========

  * *note inw: inw, -- get the initial term or ideal with respect to a weight
    vector
* Menu:

* setHomSwitch::                toggle the use of homogeneous Weyl algebra

